If you're a new player, wait for a sale. If you're a returning player from FM14/15/16, this isn't worth full price, maybe at a steep discount or if you're really dedicated to the new features (upgraded transfers, In-Game Editor, better keepers, AI Fantasy Draft), otherwise I'd avoid.
+ Excellent menu design compared to FM16, which was quite complex compared to 17, which has been majorly improved to see more in the same menu;
+ Updated players database;
+ Steam Workshop integration is, as always, a more than welcome addition, and in some cases, even necessary (lack of licenses, shown below);
+ In-Game Editor has been revamped to add an availability to get or send players out with a loan, to bind a transfer fee to players instead of sending them out with a free transfer;
+ Keepers have been given an upgrade, with more saves and more movements, instead of being a sand bag standing in their goal. Now they can actually do something;
+ Better loading times (between matches, creating or loading a new game);
+ Upgraded main menu (your manager is now universal, you can use it in every game mode and it's really easy to use, you can even get a 3D model of your face!);
+ Fantasy Draft is no longer only against human players, but you can also include AI!;
= Licensing issues in several competitions (changed or shortened off club names (A. Madrid instead of Atletico Madrid, no logo's), thanks EA;
= The Editor (not In-Game) still lacks explanation for new users;
= Press conferences are still really bland, and lack depth, I always hand them over to my assistant manager, just because it's always the same. Thankfully, that option is there, as well as an option to turn them off in the Editor;
- The price-to-features ratio is idiotic. 55€ for a revamped FM16 with a couple of new or changed features, for the majority of the people playing on older versions, this is NOT worth the money;
- Still lots of bugs in the match engine (in the Instant Result menu, there's a bug in the IR feature which causes the assistant manager to place players all over the place (for example, strikers as defenders, while I've clearly set them up as strikers, wth?);
- Input from users/gamers to SI have known to be worthless, they implement some new features, but by far not all of them.
This game falls under two categories:
then this could be worth it. I would hold off for  sale if I were you, unless you're desperate to play some FM, and believe me, it's good, it's just not worth the full price they're asking. 30-35€ would be much more reasonable.
then this is not worth the upgrade/55€ they're asking. It has some new features, but it's like CoD, it's almost the same thing every year, except for some small upgrades/changes that usally end up pretty good. However, if you own FM14/15/16, I would wait another year, unless you're dedicated to the new features or updated playerbase.
For now, I'd give this a "mixed", but since that's not available, for this price, I am giving this one a thumbs down.
A: Must have;
B: Great game, worth it, either with or without a discount;
D: Not worth it, unless it's majorly discounted;
E: Terrible title/not finished/broken, do not pick this title up under any circumstances;
F: Early Access, usual caveats/warnings apply;

Evoland will forever hold a spot in my top favorite RPGs of all time. While the game certainly moves you along a linear storyline, it isn't overly forceful when it doesn't have to be. Of course the art direction in this game is incredible. The nostalgia one endures when playing the first few levels is breathtaking. Think of it this way;
There was once a great and original RPG. It was known throughout the world to be the best RPG ever made, and all others were inferior to it. This is not that RPG. It's just a tribute...
Four stars.

